<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  RM_Venomvine.xml                           VENOMVINE_CONTACT_VENOM_BUILD_1

  The desert form of the venomvine — the first of desert.md SS4b's two "shade
  plants, which defend", the one that "holds territory by injury". Design and
  every number below: design/Jawa/worldbuilding/desert_shade_plants_design.md
  SS1d. Frozen sheets behind it: biomes/desert.md SS4b/SS6/SS9 and
  biomes/arid_shrubland.md SSVenomvine ("Desert lineage", "a scratch carries
  venom with serious results", "nearly impossible to cut down", "resist fire
  greatly").

  It lives in mandrake.rm.environmentalhazards, not beside RSW_Ultracactus in
  SWBestiary, because the defName has to carry the tier prefix of the mod it
  ships in (NAMING_SCHEME_PLAN.md SS6, checked by naming_lint.py) and nothing
  about this plant is Star Wars content. RUT_Desert wires it in with
  MayRequire, the same pattern that file already uses for every SWBestiary
  plant.

  The mechanism is CompProperties_ContactVenom on this def and nothing else:
  the comp registers this cell with MapComponent_ContactVenom, which sweeps
  for pawns standing in registered cells every 15 ticks. Nothing here ticks —
  PlantBase is tickerType Long (every 2000 ticks) and a pawn crosses a cell
  in 10-20, so a comp watching its own cell would miss essentially every
  crossing.

  Deliberately NOT built here (do not "fix" either):
    - Cutting from an adjacent cell is SAFE. JobDriver_PlantWork paths with
      PathEndMode.Touch (MEASURED), so a colonist ordered to cut a vine
      stands next to it. A cutter is only scratched when the vine's every
      neighbour is also vine — clear a stand from the edge inward. That is
      the intended player verb.
    - The shrubland thicket's "small creatures pass through easily; larger
      ones simply cannot" is body-size passability. It is BUILT, and it is
      built on the SIBLING def at the bottom of this file
      (RM_VenomvineThicket, VENOMVINE_FORTRESS_PASSABILITY_1) — never on
      RM_Venomvine, because the desert's own use of this plant is scattered
      stands ("strange vines and thorny venom writhing around some areas"),
      not a fortress. Do not add RM_CompProperties_BodySizeBarrier here.

  PLANT_GROWTH_SPEC.md's planetary x4 growth postfix applies on top of
  growDays 8 — the 8 is authored against that, not instead of it.

  Art: searched infrastructure/artpipe/{done,_artsrc,registry.jsonl,
  art_status.json} for vine/thorn/venom/bramble/briar on 2026-09-20 (re-run
  this pass, per CLAUDE.md "Check for existing regenerated art before queuing
  more"). Every hit is a DONOR plant regenerated for another biome's roster
  under ART_REGEN_FLORA_WAVE1_QUEUE_1 — ripthorn_v1, tropicalchokevine_v1,
  firevine*, brambles_v1, twistingthorn*, crystaltipbrambles_* — and none of
  them is ours or this plant. Job rmvenomvine_v1 filed this pass; the texPath
  below is where that render lands and validate_patch.py flagging it as
  pending is expected until the artpipe daemon finishes it.
-->
<Defs>

  <!-- ParentName is PlantBaseNonEdible, not PlantBase. MEASURED live 2026-09-21:
       `Config error in RM_Venomvine: Nutrition == 0 but preferability is RawBad instead
       of NeverForNutrition`. Core's PlantBase exists ONLY to add
       `<ingestible><foodType>Plant</foodType><preferability>RawBad</preferability>` on top
       of PlantBaseNonEdible (Data/Core/Defs/ThingDefs_Plants/Plants_Bases.xml lines 37-42,
       read 2026-09-21 — it adds nothing else), and this plant is deliberately Nutrition 0
       ("Nothing grazes it", below). Vanilla's own idiom for a plant nothing eats is to
       parent off PlantBaseNonEdible, so that is what this does rather than overriding the
       inherited preferability back down. -->
  <ThingDef ParentName="PlantBaseNonEdible">
    <defName>RM_Venomvine</defName>
    <label>venomvine</label>
    <description>A low, matted tangle of rust-dark runners that grows nowhere except the ground worth having. It does not compete for the shade so much as hold it: every runner carries short recurved thorns along its underside, angled to catch an ankle, and the sap they carry does not kill quickly enough to be a defence against anything but staying. Walk through a stand and you pay once. Lie down in one and you keep paying.</description>
    <statBases>
      <MaxHitPoints>300</MaxHitPoints>
      <!-- arid_shrubland.md: "resist fire greatly and burn only grudgingly";
           desert.md SS6.5 bans a local fire ecology outright. -->
      <Flammability>0.1</Flammability>
      <!-- Nothing grazes it. -->
      <Nutrition>0</Nutrition>
      <Beauty>-4</Beauty>
    </statBases>
    <graphicData>
      <texPath>Things/Plant/RM_Venomvine</texPath>
      <graphicClass>Graphic_Random</graphicClass>
      <!-- Dark rust-umber, thorn tips lighter. NO GREEN: desert.md SS9 gives
           the biome only two greens, the pale ultracactus and the dew-line
           halo, and this is neither. -->
      <color>(112, 68, 48)</color>
    </graphicData>
    <selectable>true</selectable>
    <!-- A tree is 42. At 60 the pathfinder threads the gaps in a sparse
         stand and detours a band whenever the way round is cheaper, so
         "strange vines around some areas" stays route-avoidable and only a
         stand that actually walls a patch charges a scratch. -->
    <pathCost>60</pathCost>
    <plant>
      <!-- Patch ground only. MEASURED (Terrain_Natural.xml): Sand is 0.10 and
           SoftSand 0, Gravel 0.7, Soil 1.0 — so at 0.5 this can never reach
           the sand the ultracactus holds, which is desert.md SS4b's partition
           between the two of them, in engine terms. -->
      <fertilityMin>0.5</fertilityMin>
      <fertilitySensitivity>0.3</fertilitySensitivity>
      <growDays>8</growDays>
      <!-- 0 = no age limit. A stand is permanent until something cuts it. -->
      <lifespanDaysPerGrowDays>0</lifespanDaysPerGrowDays>
      <dieIfLeafless>false</dieIfLeafless>
      <!-- Stands, not lawn. -->
      <wildClusterRadius>4</wildClusterRadius>
      <wildClusterWeight>6</wildClusterWeight>
      <wildOrder>2</wildOrder>
      <maxMeshCount>1</maxMeshCount>
      <visualSizeRange>0.8~1.2</visualSizeRange>
      <topWindExposure>0.1</topWindExposure>
      <!-- "Nearly impossible to cut down" — harvestWork is the work a cut
           costs as well as a harvest (JobDriver_PlantWork reads it for
           both). No harvestedThingDef: the desert form yields nothing.
           Dead-venomvine as a fuel industry is the SHRUBLAND sheet's own
           later work, not this. -->
      <harvestWork>900</harvestWork>
      <!-- A stand is a hazard the player clears on purpose, never something
           a growing-zone auto-cut sweep walks a colonist into. -->
      <allowAutoCut>false</allowAutoCut>
      <purpose>Misc</purpose>
    </plant>
    <comps>
      <li Class="RimMandrake.EnvironmentalHazards.CompProperties_ContactVenom">
        <damageDef>RM_VenomvineScratch</damageDef>
        <damageAmount>3</damageAmount>
        <armorPenetration>0.05</armorPenetration>
        <!-- One in-game hour: one scratch on first contact, then one per
             hour for as long as the pawn stays in the stand. -->
        <contactIntervalTicks>2500</contactIntervalTicks>
        <!-- Thorns take the legs. -->
        <bodyHeight>Bottom</bodyHeight>
      </li>
    </comps>
  </ThingDef>

  <!--
    VENOMVINE_FORTRESS_PASSABILITY_1 — the SHRUBLAND lineage of the same
    plant: arid_shrubland.md §4 "Venomvine — the fortress flora".

    Why a sibling def rather than a comp added to RM_Venomvine above: one
    ThingDef, two biomes, two different jobs. The desert form is scattered
    ("strange vines and thorny venom writhing around some areas") and must
    keep behaving exactly as it did before this item — its own ledger entry
    says so in as many words. The shrubland form is the one the sheet calls
    a fortress and a dungeon, and it is dense, harder to kill, and gated by
    body size. Everything the two share (the contact-venom mechanism, the
    numbers on it, the art, the fire resistance, Nutrition 0) is written the
    same in both; everything that differs is what this def exists for.

    Art: deliberately the SAME texPath as RM_Venomvine. The artpipe job
    rmvenomvine_v1's own style_notes were written to double as "its
    shrubland sibling" (checked 2026-09-21 per CLAUDE.md "Check for existing
    regenerated art before queuing more") — so this def is what that note
    was anticipating and NO new art job is owed for it. If the two forms are
    ever to read differently on screen, that is a future art decision, not a
    missing asset.

    PLANT_GROWTH_SPEC.md's planetary x4 growth postfix applies on top of
    growDays 12 — the 12 is authored against that, not instead of it.
  -->
  <ThingDef ParentName="PlantBaseNonEdible">
    <defName>RM_VenomvineThicket</defName>
    <label>venomvine thicket</label>
    <description>The shrubland lineage of the venomvine, and the only thing on this plain that grows in a mass. Where the desert form scatters, this one closes: runner over runner over runner until the tangle is chest-high, matted into itself, and lightless inside. Its thorns carry the same sap. A womprat runs the tangle at speed and a person can force a way in if they are patient and do not mind bleeding; nothing bigger gets past the first arm's length of it. The settlements plant it instead of building walls.</description>
    <statBases>
      <!-- "Nearly impossible to cut down", and denser than the desert form:
           the fortification reading in §7 ("living venomvine — transplantable
           fortification") only works if a stand outlasts a raid. -->
      <MaxHitPoints>500</MaxHitPoints>
      <!-- arid_shrubland.md §6.9: no flammable living flora. The dead,
           desiccated form burning hot is the smother-craft industry, which is
           not this def. -->
      <Flammability>0.1</Flammability>
      <!-- Nothing grazes it. -->
      <Nutrition>0</Nutrition>
      <Beauty>-4</Beauty>
    </statBases>
    <graphicData>
      <texPath>Things/Plant/RM_Venomvine</texPath>
      <graphicClass>Graphic_Random</graphicClass>
      <!-- §9: "venomvine as near-black islands" — the same rust-umber as the
           desert form, taken down toward black for the shrubland palette. -->
      <color>(78, 47, 33)</color>
    </graphicData>
    <selectable>true</selectable>
    <!-- Above the desert form's 60. This is the cost EVERY pawn pays for the
         cell; the body-size gate below is a separate thing layered on top of
         it, not a replacement for it, so a small creature still finds a
         thicket slower going than open ground — just not impossible. -->
    <pathCost>90</pathCost>
    <plant>
      <!-- Same partition as the desert form: patch ground only, never sand. -->
      <fertilityMin>0.5</fertilityMin>
      <fertilitySensitivity>0.3</fertilitySensitivity>
      <!-- Slower than the desert form's 8. A thicket is the work of years —
           "my grandmother darkened this stand" — and the smother-craft that
           kills one only pays because replacing it is slow. -->
      <growDays>12</growDays>
      <!-- 0 = no age limit. A stand is permanent until something kills it. -->
      <lifespanDaysPerGrowDays>0</lifespanDaysPerGrowDays>
      <dieIfLeafless>false</dieIfLeafless>
      <!-- 🔑 The dungeon, in engine terms. arid_shrubland.md §6.6 bans dense
           flora in this biome with exactly one carve-out, and this is it —
           so the clustering is deliberately far past the desert form's 4/6:
           a wide radius and a heavy weight are what make WildPlantSpawner
           produce solid islands with interiors rather than the biome's
           quincunx spacing. The maze is the cluster; no TerrainDef or
           structure is involved. -->
      <wildClusterRadius>8</wildClusterRadius>
      <wildClusterWeight>25</wildClusterWeight>
      <wildOrder>2</wildOrder>
      <maxMeshCount>1</maxMeshCount>
      <visualSizeRange>0.9~1.3</visualSizeRange>
      <topWindExposure>0.1</topWindExposure>
      <!-- Harder to cut than the desert form's 900, for the same reason
           MaxHitPoints is higher. -->
      <harvestWork>1400</harvestWork>
      <!-- A stand is cleared on purpose, never by a growing-zone auto-cut
           sweep that walks a colonist into it. -->
      <allowAutoCut>false</allowAutoCut>
      <purpose>Misc</purpose>
    </plant>
    <comps>
      <!-- Identical to the desert form's, on purpose: item point 3 — a
           creature small enough to thread the thicket is still scratched by
           it. "Small enough to pass" must never quietly mean "immune". -->
      <li Class="RimMandrake.EnvironmentalHazards.CompProperties_ContactVenom">
        <damageDef>RM_VenomvineScratch</damageDef>
        <damageAmount>3</damageAmount>
        <armorPenetration>0.05</armorPenetration>
        <contactIntervalTicks>2500</contactIntervalTicks>
        <bodyHeight>Bottom</bodyHeight>
      </li>
      <!-- "Small creatures pass through easily; larger ones simply cannot."
           The three bands, and where each number comes from, are documented
           on RM_CompProperties_BodySizeBarrier — in short: 1.5 is vanilla's
           own "this pawn fills the cell" constant (PawnUtility.PawnsCanShare
           CellBecauseOfBodySize), which puts every huge grazer on the wrong
           side of the wall, and 0.8 is ours, set so the womprat/shyrack/
           scurrier runway floor crosses free while humanlikes thread. -->
      <li Class="RimMandrake.EnvironmentalHazards.RM_CompProperties_BodySizeBarrier">
        <passFreelyBodySize>0.8</passFreelyBodySize>
        <blockBodySize>1.5</blockBodySize>
        <threadMoveCost>300</threadMoveCost>
        <trappedMoveCost>450</trappedMoveCost>
      </li>
    </comps>
    <!-- LEANINGSCRUB_RM_MOD_BUILD_1: lets mandrake.rm.leaningscrub's own Mod
         Settings switch this def's body-size barrier off ON THAT BIOME
         SPECIFICALLY, via the RM_MechanicGates seam (RM_MechanicGates.cs's
         own header: "the seam that lets a biome mod's own Mod Settings
         switch off a mechanic whose C# lives in THIS shared assembly").
         An unregistered or absent gate always reads enabled, so this is a
         no-op for every other consumer of this def (there are none today)
         and does not change default behaviour here either. -->
    <modExtensions>
      <li Class="RimMandrake.EnvironmentalHazards.RM_MechanicGateExtension">
        <gateKey>leaningscrub.venomvinePassability</gateKey>
      </li>
    </modExtensions>
  </ThingDef>

</Defs>
